# Hostile audit — half-matching upgrade for mixed flower splices

**Date:** 2026-09-15\
**Audited source:** `research/target_c_mixed_flower_half_matching_upgrade_2026-09-15.md`\
**Verdict:** **PASS at proof-only asymptotic scope.**\
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The upgrade replaces the always-safe greedy `L/3` splice matching by
`L/2-o(D)`.  The only way a degree-two graph loses from one half is through cycle
components, so the geometric burden is to prove that every splice cycle is long when
the local paired-phase mismatch is small.

## 1. A through edge preserves, rather than reverses, the oriented phase lift

At one local through flower, the two terminal boundary contacts have opposite oriented
side signs but the same projective train direction in the limit.  Choose the same
oriented projective representative `u` for both train lines.  At the `+u` boundary
side, the positive `Ju` train orientation approaches the face; at the `-u` side it
leaves the face.  The connector therefore continues the positive-normal orientation
without flipping `u` to `-u`.

The finite near-phase case has the same sign by continuity.  This is exactly the
"through, not U-turn" part of the local theorem.  Thus an edge of the splice graph
relates two oriented train representatives differing by `o(1)`, not by `pi+o(1)`.

## 2. A train used at both endpoints contributes one incoming and one outgoing splice

Fix an oriented representative `u_tau` on one stopped train.  Canonical train
orientation gives a midpoint path strictly increasing in the `Ju_tau` coordinate.
Its initial terminal is its tail and its final terminal its head.  If both endpoints
are selected splice faces, one selected connector leaves the tail and the other enters
the head.  Consequently the directed splice graph has indegree and outdegree at most
one at every train vertex.

An underlying component of degree two is therefore either a directed cycle or a
directed path after a global reversal of the component.  There is no vertex at which
two through connectors both enter the same train end.

## 3. Short-cycle phase holonomy cannot flip the lift

Propagate the oriented lift around a directed cycle.  Each splice changes the lifted
angle by at most `epsilon_D`.  If the cycle has length `ell` with

\[
ell\epsilon_D<\pi/12,
\]

all propagated representatives lie in one ordinary angular interval of width below
`pi/12`.  Returning to the antipodal lift of the first train would require angular
change close to `pi`, impossible on this interval.  Thus the final edge uses the same
lift and the positive-normal orientation is globally coherent around a short cycle.

This addresses the only possible projective-circle sign obstruction.

## 4. Positive projection excludes every short cycle

With coherent lifts, all train normals on the short cycle lie within `pi/12` of the
first normal.  Train midpoint segments lie within `pi/6` of their local normals;
through connectors lie within `pi/6+o(1)`.  Every segment of the closed directed
concatenation is therefore within `pi/4+o(1)` of one fixed normal and has strictly
positive projection on it.

The vector sum of a closed polygonal traversal is zero, so it cannot have every segment
with positive projection on one vector.  Hence a cycle must have length at least
`pi/(12 epsilon_D)` up to the irrelevant strict-endpoint convention.

The argument does not require the cycle to be simple.  It uses only total displacement.

## 5. The number of cycle components is `o(D)`

Distinct cycle components use disjoint train vertices.  Since the exact stopped-train
count is `O(D)`, the lower cycle length gives

\[
\#\text{cycles}=O(\epsilon_DD)=o(D).
\]

If `epsilon_D=0` on a subsequence, the positive-projection argument excludes cycles
entirely there, which is even stronger.

## 6. Componentwise matching loses only one half-edge per odd cycle

Paths and even cycles have matching density at least one half.  An odd cycle of `e`
edges has maximum matching `(e-1)/2`, losing only `1/2` from `e/2`.  A two-edge
parallel component has matching one, exactly half its edges.

Therefore, even charging every cycle component rather than only odd ones,

\[
P_D\ge L_D/2-c_D/2=L_D/2-o(D).
\]

The companion combinatorial regression checks the path/cycle matching formulas and the
quadratic propagation.

## 7. The deficit upgrade uses the already-audited matching theorem only

No new supertrain crossing claim is introduced here.  Once a matching of size `P_D`
is selected, the hostile-audited mixed-splice theorem gives

\[
T_D^2/4-R_D\ge P_D^2/2-o(D^2).
\]

Substituting `P_D=L_D/2-o(D)` yields `L_D^2/8-o(D^2)`.

The upgrade still does not claim that all local splices can be applied simultaneously;
long phase-winding cycles are explicitly allowed.  It uses only a matching.

**Final verdict:** PASS at proof-only asymptotic scope.  The half-matching constant is
valid for the oriented through-pair population supplied by the three-dominant flower
normal form.
